{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:57818721247e37a4e6ed16c445889327cc31ba4a9fadb1ccc1aa9fbd6087ecff","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.15},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.2},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.4}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"STARTING LEVEL, agreed by all five within 5 bp. Locked: US 10y TIPS ~2.60% (FRED DFII10, 30 Oct 2026). G7 market-GDP weights US ~0.50, Japan ~0.11, Germany ~0.11, UK ~0.08, France ~0.08, Italy ~0.06, Canada ~0.05 against reals of roughly 2.60 / 0.30 / 0.65 / 1.40 / 1.15 / 1.90 / 1.60 gives ~1.82%. The US supplies ~1.30 pp of that, so the question is ~72% a US question IN THE LEVEL. Limitation carried intact from the Scout: no non-US G7 benchmark is independently verified at the horizon — a Reuters item of 1 Sep 2026 says the 10y JGB \"rose to a 30-year high\" without giving a level, a 2.06% JGB print was described as a 26-year high in Dec-2025, and he correctly refused to rely on an UNDATED 2.635% bund level.\n\nSTRUCTURE ADOPTED: THE FERMI LADDER, WHICH IS THE BEST SINGLE PIECE OF WORK IN THE EFFORT. Delta r* nets to approximately ZERO: life-cycle dissaving +0.35 (old-age ratio 31% -> 39-41%) and public dissaving +0.25 (debt >100% by 2029, ~142% US / ~127% China by 2031) and AI/grid capital demand +0.15 (already probability-weighted at 0.34 x 0.45, small precisely because AI-Q4 withdraws that demand inside the window), against precautionary saving -0.35, cognitive-labour compression -0.10 (AI-Q5 0.382 x -0.30) and lower g -0.35 (my 1.9% coupled against a historical ~2.6% at a 0.5-0.7 pass-through). The non-obvious result stands and I keep it: CQ4 IS NOT AN r* STORY. Real term premium widens +0.40 pp, on 2A's own observables — eroding sovereign convenience yields, a marginal duration holder who is a price-SENSITIVE leveraged basis-trade account rather than a price-insensitive reserve manager, 3-year P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% of GDP and +2.4 pp more under a 20% AI-led equity valuation shock, and an equity-bond correlation flipping positive so duration that stops hedging must pay for itself.\n\nWHERE I DOWN-WEIGHTED, BY NAME. The HISTORIAN is worth near zero here: his P50 rests on what he himself calls his \"single strongest justification\", that today's level coincides with the 1960-2025 unconditional mean, while his own Reference Class 2 states the base rate of a decade staying within +/-50 bp of its start is only 25-30%. He priced the mode of a distribution he had just shown to be bimodal, and he is the only seat whose CQ4 is anchored on a coincidence.\n\nTHE BAYESIAN'S CORRECTION, WHICH I ADOPT IN FULL — the most valuable single move in the whole effort. The locked 2.60% TIPS print is EVIDENCE, not scenery, at roughly 3:1 for an elevated-real-rate five-year window, because real yields are highly persistent at a five-to-six-year horizon and 2.60% is the highest sustained US real yield since 2008. The arithmetic consequence kills the Historian's P10 of 0.40: a FIVE-YEAR AVERAGE at 0.40% requires the G7-weighted real yield to fall ~140 bp from the start AND stay there, which requires the US collapsing to ~0.5% real AND no Japanese or German normalization whatsoever. P10 moves to 1.15.\n\nTHE TWO RED-TEAM CORRECTIONS, BOTH REAL HITS. (1) NON-US CONVERGENCE AS A DELTA TERM, WHICH NO WAVE-1 SEAT SIZED. Japan, Germany, France and Italy are ~0.365 of the G7 GDP weight at 0.30-1.90% real with the 10y JGB at a 30-year high and the BoJ exiting; convergence toward 1.6-2.0% real is +0.45 to +0.55 pp on the weighted average WITH THE US UNCHANGED. This is why CQ4 is only \"72% a US question\" in the LEVEL and much less so in the DELTA. I take +0.20 pp net, not the full +0.50: part is already inside the Fermi G7-wide term premium, and the euro area has structural reasons (demographics, savings surplus, ECB reaction function) to sit below the US indefinitely. (2) THE REPRESSION-WEDGE MECHANISM CHECK. All three Wave-1 seats imported the Reinhart-Sbrancia base rate without asking whether its machinery is installed: 1945-1980 repression ran on CAPTIVE domestic banking systems with closed capital accounts and a central-bank bid, whereas the 2030s G7 has open capital accounts, a leveraged hedge-fund marginal buyer and index-constrained foreign ownership. REAL HIT. I cut the wedge from Fermi's -0.20 to -0.14 (0.30 probability x -0.45 pp) rather than the red-teamer's -0.10, because regulatory-captive duration demand (LCR, Solvency II, pension matching) is genuinely installed and the BoJ's YCC is a live MODERN instance of the mechanism, not an artifact of 1945.\n\nP50 = 1.82 - 0.05 (net Delta r*) + 0.40 (term premium) + 0.20 (non-US convergence, net) - 0.14 (repression) = 2.23 -> 2.2%.\n\nBAND. P90 = 3.4: fiscal dominance with a term-premium repricing, Japan fully normalized, no repression. I go above the Wave-1 ceiling of 3.1-3.3 because the red-teamer's netting argument is correct and nobody else made it — the three Wave-1 seats all scaled their right tail down on the ground that the 1980s started from a higher inflation base, but the 1980s ALSO started from far lower debt/GDP and a price-INSENSITIVE holder base, which cuts the other way and they did not net it. I stop at 3.4 rather than his 3.6 because a five-year average mechanically compresses tails. P10 = 1.15: regime D plus repression, AI-capex-bust deflation, Japan and euro reals pinned.\n\nCOHERENCE CHECK WITH CQ3 (r - g ARITHMETIC), EXPLICIT. My CQ4 P50 is 2.2% real; the relevant g is G7 real growth of ~1.1-1.3%, not the PPP-weighted OECD+China 1.9%. r - g = +0.9 to +1.1 pp, which matches 2A's locked \"+1.0 pp worsening in global (r - g)\" almost exactly, and that is the arithmetic by which debt/GDP keeps rising WITHOUT a growth cure — precisely what CQ6-B's \"steep real term premia\" describes and what CQ6-A's \"debt/GDP stabilizes via growth\" clause denies. My +0.40 pp term-premium widening IS B's literal language, which is coherent with B being modal at 43.\n\nSIMULATION: NOT IDENTIFIED, zero point estimate. capital.interestRate is an economy-wide real return on capital (an MPK, 5.99-6.96% across scenarios), there is no 10y sovereign benchmark, no termPremium anywhere in src/, no breakeven or inflation swap. The only content used is the cross-scenario ORDERING of debtRiskPremium (tech-breakthrough lowest at 1.20 pp, debt-populism highest at 2.50 pp with the real rate at 6.96%) as weak support for an upper-half rather than a lower-half answer, and nothing else. Low-fertility-conditional, and on this window that conditioning is growth-flattering and therefore yield-flattering UP.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.872Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the load-bearing plank of my entire payload. CQ2 falls from 44 to ~26 (conjunct A from 0.76 to ~0.48). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 27 to ~16, D from 12 to ~22, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix, probability mass shifts from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional row (-6.5 in C) toward my best (+6.0 in D), and cuts gold's weighted contribution hard. This is the cheapest single observation that falsifies me, and it is a measurement question, not a macro one.\",\"signpost\":\"The OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% y/y for four consecutive quarters, with Türkiye's core in single digits and Mexico's below 3.5% — i.e. the five EM members do NOT hold the aggregate above the G7 core.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Refutes the MOVE level-scaling argument EMPIRICALLY rather than theoretically, which is the whole of my move above the Wave-1 centre. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~14 (conjunct A from 0.50 back to ~0.30). CQ4 P90 falls from 3.4 to ~3.0 on a smaller term-premium tail. CQ6-C falls ~3 points to B. Gold's C-column number falls ~1.5 pp and long nominal sovereigns improve ~1.0 pp in every column.\",\"signpost\":\"The ICE BofA MOVE Index three-year average over 2027-2029, taken from ICE's own index data rather than an aggregator, prints below 90 WHILE the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yield averages above 3.75% over the same three years.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"IMF WEO October 2029 edition\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the measured-versus-true-China channel that is worth ~0.3 pp of my CQ3 P50 and ~5 points of my CQ6-A, and is the main place I moved toward the red-team. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.9 to ~1.7 and P90 from 2.9 to ~2.7; CQ6-A falls from 18 to ~12 with the mass going to B (+5) and D (+1). China equities/onshore duration: the equity half is damaged in every column and the duration half improves. It would also partly vindicate the Bayesian seat's narrower P90 of 2.65 against mine.\",\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO prints combined 2030 and 2031 China real GDP growth below 3.5%/yr on the REALIZED/ESTIMATE line (not the forecast line) — i.e. the official series stops printing near its target.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies BOTH places I departed upward from the Wave-1 CQ4 centre: the non-US-convergence delta term (+0.20 pp) and my cut of the Reinhart-Sbrancia repression wedge from -0.20 to -0.14. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.2 to ~1.4 and P90 from 3.4 to ~2.9; P10 falls from 1.15 to ~0.6, restoring the repression tail that the 3:1 TIPS-anchor likelihood ratio currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~17 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. CQ6-D rises from 12 to ~19. G7 long nominals, TIPS and cash are all mis-priced too low in my 2026-2030 buildout column.\",\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal minus 10y inflation swap) averages below 1.00% for twelve consecutive months, with the 10y JGB real yield still below 0.50%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the physical-energization cap that holds my CQ3 P90 at 2.9 and that makes power/grid (+7.5) and construction/trades (+6.5) my two best 2026-2030 rows. CQ3 P90 rises to ~3.2 and P50 to ~2.0; CQ6-A rises from 18 to ~24 with B falling. My upward correction to AI-Q4 (toward 0.73) reverses toward the locked 0.66, and realized AI-Q3 load returns toward the locked 998 TWh P50. In the matrix, power/grid buildout falls from 7.5 to ~4.5 and construction from 6.5 to ~4.0 as the scarcity rent evaporates, while the AI-complex column rises ~3 pp in A and B.\",\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks AND gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting — against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'growth'."}}